WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:07.440
Speaker 1: Is it good that many of the most&nbsp;
powerful federal elected officials in the United&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:13.840
States are in their 80s or even close to their&nbsp;
90s in some cases? Now, let me set this up for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:19.200
you. Last week, Republican Senator Chuck Grassley&nbsp;
announced he's going to be running for reelection.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:30.160
He is 88. Senate terms are six years long. We're&nbsp;
talking 90s. If he wins, Joe Biden is almost 79.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:37.520
Nancy Pelosi, speaker of the House is 81. Steny&nbsp;
Hoyer is 82. Jim Clyburn is 81. Those are the top&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:41.760
three Democrats in the House of Representatives.&nbsp;
Over in the Senate, the Democratic leader,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:48.640
Chuck Schumer, is only 70. Republican leader Mitch&nbsp;
McConnell, 79 years old. Senator Dianne Feinstein,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:56.000
88 years old. Bernie is 80 years old. Patrick&nbsp;
Leahy is 81. Jim Inhofe is 86. I can give you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:02.080
the list. Fine. This is not about ageism.&nbsp;
Republicans have actually been using ageist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:07.520
tropes against Joe Biden. Joe Biden's barely&nbsp;
older than Donald Trump. They're big alpha boy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:14.720
strong hero. Joe Biden's barely older Republicans&nbsp;
have been using a lot of ageist tropes against Joe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:21.360
Biden. I have no interest in like just a generally&nbsp;
ageist discussion. It's more about is this country&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:29.120
well represented if our elected officials skew so&nbsp;
dramatically older than the average population?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:34.880
Now, to some degree, it makes sense. Five year&nbsp;
olds contribute to the average population,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:39.280
but five year olds can't be senators. So like to&nbsp;
some degree, it would make sense that our elected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:43.920
officials are older than the average population,&nbsp;
but we're talking about dramatically so. We've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.920 --> 00:01:48.720
also talked before about whether there should be&nbsp;
an upper age limit for president. For example.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:55.040
This isn't about, you know, imposing anything.&nbsp;
Some people want an upper age limit. This is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.040 --> 00:02:00.400
more about whether it could be argued that&nbsp;
the population of the United States overall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:09.280
is well served and well represented by folks who&nbsp;
skew dramatically older. There is a strong case to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:15.280
be made that the answer is no. Now, at the same&nbsp;
time, we want people who are more experienced.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:21.440
Oftentimes people get good at their jobs after&nbsp;
they've been in the job for a while. You can also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:26.160
have corruption and rot, which sets in when&nbsp;
people have been in their jobs for a while.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:32.240
We want people who know how these legislative&nbsp;
bodies work and know how to get things done. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:37.760
if you increase turnover dramatically in the&nbsp;
House and Senate, you probably increase this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:44.000
revolving door of lobbyists and a number of other&nbsp;
problems. Now, aside from an age limit, you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:49.040
also look at term limits. But there's quite a bit&nbsp;
of literature in terms of term limits, which says&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:56.080
term limits create corruption and they create&nbsp;
incompetence because you force out lots of people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.640 --> 00:03:02.800
who could be doing their job really well. They&nbsp;
force people out as they become more experienced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:07.200
and you create the opportunity for moneyed&nbsp;
interests to interfere and swoop in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:14.960
write legislation. It's a complicated issue for&nbsp;
sure. The idea of an age limit is not unheard of.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:20.160
Many people don't know that Canada has a&nbsp;
maximum age for senators. There's mandatory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:26.160
retirement for Canadian senators at age&nbsp;
seventy five. So then we get at the end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:32.800
to turnout is really low in the United States&nbsp;
without. Changing anything about the rules,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:39.600
term limits, maximum age or any of this stuff,&nbsp;
if more people wanted younger elected officials,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:44.640
they could get out and vote for them, support&nbsp;
them in primaries, and the problem would sort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:50.800
of take care of itself. Now, many people would&nbsp;
say, hey, there's too many 80 year olds and my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:55.440
generation is not represented and I'm going to go&nbsp;
out and vote. But a lot of the people who say that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.440 --> 00:04:01.120
don't actually go out and vote, does that make&nbsp;
sense? No, but it's the situation that we have.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:06.960
So do we really need age limits? Do we really&nbsp;
need term limits or do we just need more voter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:13.120
participation and higher turnout from younger&nbsp;
people who are overwhelmingly underrepresented in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:18.720
the electorate that chooses to vote? It would make&nbsp;
sense. Like if you step back from all of this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:24.960
if older folks are more reliable voters,&nbsp;
it makes sense that you might see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:31.680
older folks also overrepresented in terms of&nbsp;
who wins. So let me know your thoughts. Is this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:37.360
question. One is, is this really an issue that&nbsp;
elected representatives, particularly in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:44.960
Senate and in general and federal government, skew&nbsp;
older? And if it is a problem, are we better off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:50.960
leaning on a solution like a term limit or an age&nbsp;
limit or this? Is this really just an issue of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.960 --> 00:04:56.560
apathy among those who casually would like&nbsp;
younger representatives but are actually going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.560 --> 00:05:00.400
out and voting in significant numbers? Let me know&nbsp;
your thoughts. Is it a problem? And what is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:06.132
solution of so you can find me on Twitter at David&nbsp;
Pakman. Remember that The David Pakman Show does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.132 --> 00:05:10.330
depend on viewers like you, listeners like you,&nbsp;
who sign up on our website and become members,&nbsp;&nbsp;

